A||||3||loafer87gt||Rocker Arm conversion questions||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||01-11-2000||09:55 PM||||Hi guys,<P>As you have probably gathered from my previous posts, I am in the process of rebuilding my 2.8 V6. I wanted to do a rocker arm conversion while the engine is out, but I have heard several members on the Fiero list say that they wear the valve guides out prematurely, in fact one guy said he got less than 3000 km out his engine after installing rocker arms before his car was smoking really bad. Does anyone know a way around this problem, as I think this would be a neat and affordable mod to due to my engine.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Harry||01-11-2000||10:29 PM||||First of all what are U installing? If you drastically change the ratio then yes you can start wearing the guides in a manner they are not used to. Of course this will lead to premature failure. If you gonna go that route better to just pull the heads then the whole nine (V.job 3 angle/new springs,guides and retainers) even valves if needed deck the heads port match intake/heads etc. etc.||||reg||10
Z||000002||batboy||01-11-2000||07:31 PM||||I'm assuming you mean the 1.6:1 ratio rocker arms. If you are rebuilding the engine, why not just install the cam with the right lift you want so you don't have to mess with different ratios on the rockers.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||01-12-2000||12:15 AM||||I agree. There are cams that will avoid the need for the higher ratio rockers although I've never run into a problems with them over many miles. 1.7's might be a bit much.<P>||||reg||1
